\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 At fifteen minutes past five on Ascension Eve, while civil twilight was just beginning to pick shapes out of the Jamison Valley, PAPER TIGER left Echo Point carrying enough weaponry to make bushwalking an unusually consequential recreational activity.\
They descended past the Three Sisters by the Giant Stairway, nearly a thousand steps which had presumably been installed by somebody with a profound dislike of knees. Each operative carried two automatic rifles, ammunition for both, provisions, climbing gear and the usual assortment of equipment required by people who intended to arrive somewhere heavily armed without using a road. They carried no communications equipment. Anything transmitting in the valley was another opportunity to tell Shakti Security that somebody was coming.\
The second rifle was not redundancy. TIGER was delivering weapons for people already going in through the front door.\
The first part of the descent was straightforward in the technical sense that there were stairs and nobody fell off them. Straightforward ceased to mean easy after several hundred metres of steel, stone, cliff and pack straps cutting into shoulders. Rifles which had seemed reassuringly compact at Echo Point became awkward metal appendages that caught on handrails, rock and scrub. Packs had to be turned sideways through tighter sections, and men and women accustomed to regarding twenty kilos as an administrative detail began regarding every additional cartridge as a personal insult.\
At the bottom they followed Dardanelles Pass beneath the cliffs towards Leura Forest. The light strengthened above them without contributing much beneath the canopy. Coachwood, sassafras and lilly pilly closed around the track, the air cooler and damper than the plateau they had left, while the sandstone wall remained intermittently visible through the trees on their left. Somewhere several hundred metres above that wall the UDF occupied roads and checkpoints and watched the obvious ways into Sublime Point.\
TIGER continued underneath them.\
Beyond Leura Forest the route became less accommodating. They moved east along the old tracks towards Lindeman Pass, picking their way through fallen timber, loose rock, creek crossings and sections which seemed to have survived mainly because the bush had not yet completed the paperwork required to reclaim them. Where packs would not pass cleanly, weapons went first and packs followed. Where neither would pass comfortably, operatives discovered there were degrees of discomfort not adequately represented in PAPER occupational health forms.\
By late morning the novelty of walking underneath the enemy had worn off.\
Nobody talked more than necessary. They drank, ate while moving or during brief halts, checked feet before blisters became injuries, redistributed ammunition when one pack began punishing its owner more enthusiastically than the others, and continued east beneath the Sublime Point escarpment towards Copeland Pass.\
The final ascent was why they had started before sunrise.\
Copeland Pass did not offer the plateau so much as negotiate with it. The route rose through broken sandstone, scrub and steep pitches beneath the cliffs, forcing the team to climb with loads designed for fighting rather than mountaineering. Rifles were slung, unslung and passed upward. Packs followed on shoulders until the terrain objected, then by hand. On sections where two people could not safely occupy the same piece of slope, the lead climbed ahead, found purchase and hauled equipment while the next operative came after.\
The valley dropped away behind them, widening through gaps in the trees until Mount Solitary and the opposite cliffs appeared less like scenery than evidence of how far down they had been.\
At 14:45, nine and a half hours after leaving Echo Point, what remained of PAPER TIGER reached the upper bush south of Sublime Point.\
No alarm greeted them.\
No Shakti patrol appeared. Nothing rose from the ground, fired from the trees or asked them to identify themselves. The southern valley-floor defences which should have made the approach considerably more exciting had failed to do so, and TIGER was not sufficiently offended to investigate why.\
They moved into quiet bush south of the townhouses, concealed themselves and waited.\
They still had the rifles.}